## How to use

Most game code only needs two things from this module:

1. **Draw ImGui from an editor tool.** ImGui calls are valid between `ImGui::NewFrame()` and `ImGui::Render()`. In Janus that is inside an editor tool's `Draw_Internal()`, not inside ECS systems, which run before the ImGui frame starts. See \ref editor.
2. **Use the shared context** if you call ImGui from code that may have its own ImGui globals (a separate DLL, for example): call `ImGui::SetCurrentContext(ImGuiWrapper::GetCurrentContext())` first. Code linked into the same executable as the engine shares the context automatically.

On start, the module turns on ImGui docking and multi-viewport (\src{src/Editor/ImGuiWrapper/Module.cpp,49,52}). With multi-viewport, window positions are in screen coordinates, and a window moved outside the game window becomes its own OS window. Place windows relative to `ImGui::GetMainViewport()->Pos`:

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,A HUD window anchored to the top-right of the game window}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp hud
\endex

## Debug factories

A *debug factory* is an object with a `DrawDebug(void*)` function, registered under a name with ImGuiWrapper::RegisterFactory(). ImGuiWrapper::DrawFactory_Container(name, pointer) then draws the data at `pointer` with the factory registered under `name`.

The Entity Debugger uses this to draw components: for each component on the selected entity, it calls `DrawFactory_Container` with the component's name. The component macros register a factory per component for you (#REGISTER_COMPONENT_DEBUGGER):

\engex{include/Gameplay/ECS/Components.hpp,21,22,How a component's panel is registered}
```cpp
#define REGISTER_COMPONENT_DEBUGGER(ComponentClass) \
    ImGuiWrapper::RegisterFactory(#ComponentClass, \
        TObjectPtr<Components::ComponentClass##Debug>())
```
\endex

To draw a component a completely different way, register your own factory under the component's name after #ECS_REGISTER_COMPONENT: a later registration replaces the earlier one.

## Things to watch out for

- ImGui calls from ECS systems or from `main()` crash or assert: there is no ImGui frame open. Use an editor tool.
- `ImGuiWrapper::TeardownModule()` is never called; the ImGui context is destroyed by Renderer::TeardownModule().
- The engine does not check `ImGui::GetIO().WantCaptureKeyboard`, so typing in an ImGui text field also triggers game input.
*/

